        "question": "A 48-year-old man is diagnosed with proximal DVT (femoral vein) after recent knee surgery. He has no contraindications to anticoagulation. Which anticoagulation strategy is most appropriate according to Australian guidelines?",
        "options": {
            "a": "Aspirin 300 mg daily for 3 months",
            "b": "Warfarin alone (no bridging)",
            "c": "Rivaroxaban 15 mg twice daily for 21 days, then 20 mg once daily",
            "d": "Apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily indefinitely",
            "e": "Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) for 5 days only, then stop"
        },
        "correct_answer": "c",
        "explanation": "For acute proximal DVT, direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) are first-line in Australia. Rivaroxaban loading is 15 mg BD × 21 days, then 20 mg daily. This is a provoked DVT (surgery trigger), so anticoagulation duration is typically 3 months. DOACs preferred over warfarin (no INR monitoring needed, faster onset). Aspirin is not adequate anticoagulation for DVT.",
        "rationale": {
            "a": "Incorrect - aspirin is antiplatelet, NOT anticoagulant; inadequate for DVT treatment (won't prevent thrombus propagation or PE)",
            "b": "Incorrect - warfarin requires INR monitoring and takes days to reach therapeutic level; DOACs preferred in Australia for convenience",
