    {
        "id": "WEEK3-RESP-111",
        "topic": "Recurrent VTE Management",
        "question": "A 52-year-old man on apixaban 5 mg twice daily for unprovoked PE (diagnosed 8 months ago) presents with new right leg swelling. Compression ultrasound confirms new proximal DVT in the right femoral vein. What is the most appropriate management?",
        "options": {
            "a": "Increase apixaban dose to 10 mg twice daily",
            "b": "Switch to warfarin (target INR 2-3)",
            "c": "Switch to low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) indefinitely",
            "d": "Check apixaban levels; increase dose if subtherapeutic",
            "e": "Assess for thrombophilia and continue current dose if no cause found"
        },
        "correct_answer": "c",
        "explanation": "Recurrent VTE ON therapeutic anticoagulation suggests anticoagulation failure. For recurrent VTE despite DOAC therapy, Australian guidelines recommend switching to LMWH (low molecular weight heparin) indefinitely. LMWH has more predictable pharmacokinetics and can be dose-adjusted based on anti-Xa levels. Investigate for underlying cause (cancer, thrombophilia, non-compliance).",
        "rationale": {
            "a": "Incorrect - no evidence that increasing DOAC dose beyond standard prevents recurrence; switch to different anticoagulant class (LMWH) preferred",
            "b": "Incorrect - warfarin also a DOAC failure; switching from one oral anticoagulant to another unlikely to help; LMWH preferred",
            "c": "CORRECT - Recurrent VTE on DOAC = anticoagulation failure → switch to LMWH indefinitely (more predictable, dose-adjustable based on anti-Xa)",
            "d": "Incorrect - DOAC levels not routinely measured or used to guide dosing; switching anticoagulant class (to LMWH) is standard approach",
            "e": "Incorrect - must change management (switch to LMWH); just assessing for cause without changing treatment inadequate"
        },
        "key_learning_points": [
            "Recurrent VTE ON therapeutic anticoagulation = treatment FAILURE",
            "Management: Switch to LMWH indefinitely (e.g., enoxaparin 1.5 mg/kg daily)",
            "LMWH advantages: Predictable pharmacokinetics, dose-adjustable (anti-Xa monitoring), no drug interactions",
            "Investigate underlying causes: Occult cancer, antiphospholipid syndrome, non-compliance, drug interactions",
            "Alternative: Increase warfarin target INR to 2.5-3.5 (if warfarin failure)",
            "Consider IVC filter if recurrent PE despite LMWH"
        ],
        "tags": ["respiratory", "VTE", "recurrent VTE", "anticoagulation failure", "LMWH", "week3"],
        "difficulty": "hard",
        "subspecialty": "Respiratory Medicine / Haematology",
        "citations": [
